@*
    One route's status card on the Overview grid.

    Layout contract
    ---------------
    Every row — source, destination, buffer — is the SAME three-column grid:

        [ icon 22px ] [ body 1fr, min-width:0 ] [ actions auto ]

    so the icons form one vertical rule down the left, the text block shares one
    left edge, and every status pill lands on one right-hand column. Each row
    used to be its own ad-hoc flex stack, which is why the source pill sat
    inboard of the route and destination pills.

    The outer element stays a MudPaper. The card's CSS specificity work — the
    shared card rule, the hover rule and the health accent — is written against
    `.mud-paper.route-card`, so swapping it for a plain <article> silently drops
    all three. Only the INSIDE is BEM.

    Visual rules (locked)
    ---------------------
       * Status dot is the focal point — green/red, shape as well as colour.
       * Route id is the headline.
       * Source + protocol + state on one line.
       * Sink(s) each get a line with their adapter state + degraded badge +
         active-session count when applicable.
       * Buffer depth as text, NOT a chart. Per the "no analytics in Studio"
         rule (locked in M plan review).
       * Last-error guidance + code + age shown when present.
       * A card that is not green ALWAYS states its reason — from the fault
         panel when an adapter reported the error, otherwise from the health
         panel. A red card with nothing to read on it is not reachable.

    Reference: docs/PHASE4_EXECUTION_PLAN.md Milestone M.1a
*@
@inject IDialogService DialogService

@if (Route is not null)
{
    <MudPaper Elevation="1" Class="@($"pa-4 route-card {HealthModifier}")" Style="height:100%;">
        <div class="route-card__header">
            @* Shape as well as colour: on a board of cards this dot is the first
               thing scanned, and it must not be the only thing carrying state. *@
            <MudIcon Icon="@OperatorFormat.StateIcon(Route.StateName)"
                     Color="@StateColor" Size="Size.Medium"
                     Class="route-card__dot" aria-hidden="true" />
            <div class="route-card__identity">
                <span class="route-card__eyebrow">Route</span>
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="route-card__title" title="@Route.RouteId">
                    @Route.RouteId
                </MudText>
            </div>
            <span class="status-pill @StatePillClass" data-testid="route-card-state">
                @StateLabel
            </span>
        </div>

        <div class="route-card__rows">
            @* ── Source ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
            @if (Route.Source is not null)
            {
                <div class="route-row">
                    <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Input" Size="Size.Small"
                             Class="route-row__icon" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <div class="route-row__body">
                        <span class="route-row__label">Source</span>
                        <a class="route-row__name" href="@($"/sources/{Route.Source.SourceInstanceId}")">
                            @Route.Source.SourceInstanceId
                        </a>
                        <span class="route-row__proto">@Route.Source.ProtocolName</span>

                        @* Always rendered, including at zero. A disconnected
                           device reads 0 values, and hiding this line exactly
                           when the count is 0 removed the evidence at the only
                           moment it mattered. The second clause separates "never
                           received anything" from "received something, but not
                           recently" — different faults. *@
                        <span class="route-row__meta">
                            @Route.Source.PointsObserved.ToString("N0") values read since start
                            ·
                            @(Route.Source.LastPointAtUtc is { } lastPointAt
                                ? $"last value {OperatorFormat.Ago(lastPointAt)}"
                                : "no values received yet")
                        </span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="route-row__actions">
                        @* Per-source diagnostic log: view + opt-in enable (records
                           connect/disconnect + data reads to logs\<source>.txt).

                           Sits BEFORE the state pill so every row's pill lands on
                           the same right-hand column. *@
                        <MudTooltip Text="Source log — view / enable logging">
                            <MudIconButton Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Article"
                                           Size="Size.Small" Color="Color.Default"
                                           Class="route-row__log"
                                           OnClick="@(() => OpenLog(Route.Source.SourceInstanceId))"
                                           aria-label="@($"Open the connection log for {Route.Source.SourceInstanceId}")"
                                           data-testid="source-log-button" />
                        </MudTooltip>
                        <span class="status-pill @PillClass(Route.Source.StateName)">
                            @Route.Source.StateName
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                <div class="route-row route-row--empty">
                    <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Input" Size="Size.Small"
                             Class="route-row__icon" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <div class="route-row__body">
                        <span class="route-row__name route-row__name--muted">No source attached</span>
                    </div>
                    @* The empty rows carry a pill too. Every other row lands one
                       on the right-hand column, so a row without one reads as
                       "state not reported yet" rather than "nothing is here" —
                       and this row is a reason the route is red, not a gap. *@
                    <div class="route-row__actions">
                        <span class="status-pill status-pill--error"
                              data-testid="route-card-no-source">not attached</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }

            @* ── Destinations ─────────────────────────────────────────── *@
            @if (Route.Sinks.Count == 0)
            {
                <div class="route-row route-row--empty">
                    <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Output" Size="Size.Small"
                             Class="route-row__icon" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <div class="route-row__body">
                        <span class="route-row__name route-row__name--muted">No destinations attached</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="route-row__actions">
                        <span class="status-pill status-pill--error"
                              data-testid="route-card-no-destination">not attached</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                @foreach (var sink in Route.Sinks)
                {
                    <div class="route-row">
                        <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Output" Size="Size.Small"
                                 Class="route-row__icon" aria-hidden="true" />
                        <div class="route-row__body">
                            <span class="route-row__label">Destination</span>
                            <a class="route-row__name" href="@($"/sinks/{sink.SinkInstanceId}")">
                                @sink.SinkInstanceId
                            </a>
                            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(sink.ProtocolName))
                            {
                                <span class="route-row__proto">@sink.ProtocolName</span>
                            }
                        </div>
                        <div class="route-row__actions">
                            @if (sink.ActiveSessionCount is { } n)
                            {
                                <MudTooltip Text="Active client sessions">
                                    <span class="status-pill status-pill--neutral">
                                        <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.People" Size="Size.Small" aria-hidden="true" />
                                        @n
                                    </span>
                                </MudTooltip>
                            }
                            @if (sink.IsDegraded)
                            {
                                <span class="status-pill status-pill--warn">degraded</span>
                            }
                            else if (sink.AdapterStateName is { } stateName)
                            {
                                <span class="status-pill @PillClass(stateName)">@stateName</span>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
            }

            @* ── Buffer ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
            @if (Route.Buffer is not null)
            {
                <div class="route-row">
                    <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Inventory2" Size="Size.Small"
                             Class="route-row__icon" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <div class="route-row__body">
                        @* Waiting AND delivered, because either number alone lies:
                           a queue of 7 is normal in flight, a queue of 7 that has
                           never delivered anything is a wedged route. The <text>
                           wrappers are load-bearing — without them the @if sits in
                           a text context and Razor prints the directive itself
                           onto the card. *@
                        <span class="route-row__buffer">
                            <strong>@Route.Buffer.CurrentDepth.ToString("N0") waiting</strong>
                            · @Route.Buffer.TotalDrained.ToString("N0") delivered
                            @if (Route.Buffer.SizeBytes > 0)
                            {
                                <text> · @OperatorFormat.Bytes(Route.Buffer.SizeBytes) on disk</text>
                            }
                            @if (Route.Buffer.TotalDropped > 0)
                            {
                                <text> · </text>
                                <span class="route-row__dropped">@Route.Buffer.TotalDropped.ToString("N0") dropped</span>
                            }
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        @* ── Faults ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        @if (Route.Source?.LastErrorAtUtc is { } errAt)
        {
            @ErrorPanel("Source", Route.Source.LastErrorCode, Route.Source.LastErrorMessage, errAt)
        }
        @foreach (var sink in Route.Sinks)
        {
            @* Destination failures surface here too (e.g. broker unreachable —
               MQTT.PERTAG_PUBLISH_FAILED). Only source errors used to reach the
               card, so a degraded destination showed no reason at all. *@
            @if (sink.LastErrorAtUtc is { } sinkErrAt)
            {
                @ErrorPanel($"Destination {sink.SinkInstanceId}", sink.LastErrorCode, sink.LastErrorMessage, sinkErrAt)
            }
        }

        @* ── Why this card is red, when no adapter raised an error ──────────
           Plenty of breakages carry no error code at all: a route with no
           destination, a source sitting Stopped, a buffer that has never
           drained. Those printed a red "Not delivering" pill and nothing else,
           leaving the operator to work out which of the rows above was the
           problem. The verdict names the exact condition that set the level, so
           a red or amber card always says why.

           FromAdapterError is the suppression test, rather than "is a fault
           panel showing?": the panels above render for ANY reported error,
           including one stale enough that it no longer decides the level. Asking
           the verdict which kind of condition it came from keeps this in step
           with the level itself — an error-derived verdict is already stated,
           word for word, by the panel above. *@
        @if (RouteHealth.Verdict(Route) is { FromAdapterError: false, Reason: { } reason })
        {
            @HealthPanel(reason)
        }
    </MudPaper>
}

@* Shared fault panel for both ends of the route.

   Leads with what the fault MEANS and what to check (ErrorGuidance), because
   the operator reading this is standing at a machine deciding whether to look
   at the cable, the PLC, or the gateway. The raw code stays as a mono chip
   beside the age, so it is still greppable in a support ticket — demoted, not
   removed. Uncatalogued codes fall back to the adapter's own message, so
   nothing is ever swallowed. *@
@code {
    // NB: the code parameter is deliberately NOT named `code` — inside a .razor
    // file `@code` is the code-block directive, so `@code` in the markup below
    // is a Razor parse error rather than a variable reference.
    private RenderFragment ErrorPanel(string origin, string? errorCode, string? rawMessage, DateTime whenUtc) => __builder =>
    {
        var explanation = ErrorGuidance.For(errorCode);

        @* Always red. The panel used to fade to amber once the error passed two
           minutes, so a device unreachable for an hour looked calmer than one
           unreachable for a minute — the longer it was broken, the less broken
           it looked, on the one screen used for triage. Age still reads in words
           on the meta line below ("since 17 sec ago" / "last seen 8 min ago"),
           which is where that distinction belongs. *@
        <div class="route-card__fault route-card__fault--active" role="status">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ErrorOutline" Size="Size.Small" aria-hidden="true" />
            <div class="route-card__fault-body">
                <p class="route-card__fault-meaning">@ErrorGuidance.Describe(errorCode, rawMessage)</p>
                @if (explanation?.WhatToCheck is { } whatToCheck)
                {
                    <p class="route-card__fault-check">@whatToCheck</p>
                }
                <div class="route-card__fault-meta">
                    @* Which END failed. A route can fan out to several
                       destinations, and an uncatalogued code falls back to the
                       adapter's raw message, which need not name the endpoint —
                       so the origin is stated rather than inferred. *@
                    <span>@origin</span>
                    @if (errorCode is not null)
                    {
                        <code class="route-card__fault-code">@errorCode</code>
                    }
                    <span>@ErrorAge(whenUtc)</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    };

    // Same panel, same red, for the breakages that never raise an error code.
    // Deliberately reuses the fault panel's classes rather than inventing a
    // quieter treatment: the card is red either way, and a softer panel would
    // say "less wrong" about a route that is delivering exactly nothing.
    //
    // The sentence is split at its first full stop so it lands in the panel's
    // existing grammar — the fact in the bold line, the consequence and the
    // "nothing is wrong with the machine" reassurance in the quieter one below.
    private RenderFragment HealthPanel(string reason) => __builder =>
    {
        var (fact, consequence) = SplitFirstSentence(reason);

        <div class="route-card__fault route-card__fault--active" role="status"
             data-testid="route-card-health">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ErrorOutline" Size="Size.Small" aria-hidden="true" />
            <div class="route-card__fault-body">
                <p class="route-card__fault-meaning">@fact</p>
                @if (consequence is not null)
                {
                    <p class="route-card__fault-check">@consequence</p>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    };
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RouteSummaryDto? Route { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Splits "fact. consequence" into its two halves; returns the whole string
    /// as the fact when it is a single sentence.
    /// </summary>
    private static (string Fact, string? Consequence) SplitFirstSentence(string reason)
    {
        var cut = reason.IndexOf(". ", StringComparison.Ordinal);
        return cut < 0
            ? (reason, null)
            : (reason[..(cut + 1)], reason[(cut + 2)..]);
    }

    private Task OpenLog(string sourceId)
    {
        var parameters = new DialogParameters<SourceLogDialog>
        {
            { x => x.SourceId, sourceId },
        };
        return DialogService.ShowAsync<SourceLogDialog>(
            $"Source log — {sourceId}",
            parameters,
            new DialogOptions { MaxWidth = MaxWidth.Medium, FullWidth = true, CloseButton = true });
    }

    // §11 — the live/stale ladder now lands in WORDS only, not in colour. A
    // fault panel is red whatever its age; this line says how old it is and
    // whether it may already have cleared.
    private static string ErrorAge(DateTime whenUtc)
    {
        if (OperatorFormat.IsStale(whenUtc, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(15)))
        {
            return $"last seen {OperatorFormat.Ago(whenUtc)} — may already be cleared";
        }
        if (OperatorFormat.IsStale(whenUtc, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2)))
        {
            return $"last seen {OperatorFormat.Ago(whenUtc)}";
        }
        return $"since {OperatorFormat.Ago(whenUtc)}";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Adapter state name to pill modifier. One mapping for the source and sink
    /// pills, so the same word can never render green in one row and grey in the
    /// next. The route's own pill goes through <see cref="StatePillClass"/> —
    /// it answers a different question (delivery, not adapter state).
    /// </summary>
    private static string PillClass(string? stateName) => stateName switch
    {
        "Running" => "status-pill--ok",
        "Degraded" => "status-pill--warn",
        "Stopped" or "Failed" or "Faulted" or "Blocked" => "status-pill--error",
        _ => "status-pill--neutral",
    };

    // Colour and label both come from RouteHealth, not from Route.StateName.
    // StateName describes the PIPELINE: a route whose source is Stopped and
    // whose sink is disconnected still reports "Running", because the pipeline
    // task is alive with nothing to read and nowhere to publish. Colouring the
    // chip from it printed a green RUNNING badge over a route moving no data.
    // Going through RouteHealth also guarantees this card and the Overview
    // banner cannot disagree in front of the operator.
    // Degraded is RED, not amber. An amber strip was read as "not urgent" and
    // skipped — a gateway with four unreachable devices still looked advisory.
    // Green has to mean everything runs; everything else is red until it does,
    // which also matches the verdict banner these cards sit under. The pill
    // TEXT still separates "Degraded" from "Not delivering", so the distinction
    // survives where it is actionable, without colour understating either one.
    private Color StateColor => RouteHealth.Level(Route) switch
    {
        RouteHealthLevel.Down or RouteHealthLevel.Degraded => Color.Error,
        _ => Color.Success,
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The route pill's colour. Deliberately NOT <see cref="PillClass"/>: this
    /// one tracks delivery via RouteHealth, so Degraded lands on --error to stay
    /// in step with the header icon and the verdict banner above the board.
    /// </summary>
    private string StatePillClass => RouteHealth.Level(Route) switch
    {
        RouteHealthLevel.Down or RouteHealthLevel.Degraded => "status-pill--error",
        _ => "status-pill--ok",
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The left accent answers "IS THE MACHINE CONNECTED?", so it reads
    /// <see cref="RouteHealth.ConnectionLevel"/> — endpoint states only — not
    /// <see cref="RouteHealth.Level"/>.
    ///
    /// Driving it from Level painted the same red edge on a gateway reading its
    /// device perfectly but unable to drain to the broker as on one whose device
    /// was unplugged, so an operator could not tell "walk to the machine" from
    /// "the broker is down" without opening every card in turn.
    ///
    /// Consequence, deliberately accepted: a green edge does not promise data is
    /// flowing, and this card can be green-edged while the Overview banner still
    /// counts the route as broken. The delivery fault stays visible on this same
    /// card — in the state pill, the fault panel and the waiting / delivered
    /// counters — so nothing is hidden. What stays forbidden is two surfaces
    /// answering the SAME question differently, which is why the pill below
    /// still speaks for delivery.
    /// </summary>
    private string HealthModifier => RouteHealth.ConnectionLevel(Route) switch
    {
        RouteHealthLevel.Down or RouteHealthLevel.Degraded => "route-card--attention",
        _ => "route-card--healthy",
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// What the pill says. "Running" is reserved for a route that is actually
    /// delivering end to end; a broken one says so in words, because a red pill
    /// reading "Running" asks the operator to reconcile the contradiction
    /// themselves. The per-endpoint pills below still show each raw state.
    /// </summary>
    private string StateLabel => RouteHealth.Level(Route) switch
    {
        RouteHealthLevel.Down => "Not delivering",
        RouteHealthLevel.Degraded => "Degraded",
        _ => Route?.StateName ?? "Unknown",
    };
}
